.TH HOARD 1 "August 2026" "hoard 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hoard \- encrypted password vault in one readable file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hoard
.RI [ \-\-vault
.IR PATH ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hoard
keeps passwords in a single file you can decrypt without
.BR hoard .
The header carrying the Argon2id parameters, the salt and the nonce is
plaintext, because you cannot derive the key without them. It is passed to the
cipher as associated data, so the parameters can be read by anyone and weakened
by nobody: editing them invalidates the authentication tag instead of producing
a cheaper key.
.PP
A fresh salt and a fresh nonce are generated on every write. Nonce reuse is how
most homemade encryption dies, so it is made structurally impossible rather than
avoided by being careful.
.PP
There is no account, no server and no synchronisation. If you forget the master
password the contents are gone, and there is no reset link.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Create a new vault. Prompts for a master password twice.
.TP
.BI add " NAME"
Add an entry. With
.B \-g
the password is generated rather than typed.
.TP
.BI edit " NAME"
Change fields on an existing entry, leaving the others alone. Passing a flag
with an empty value clears that field, which is not the same as omitting the
flag.
.TP
.BI get " NAME"
Copy the entry's password to the clipboard. It is wiped after twenty seconds,
but only if the clipboard still holds it, so copying something else in the
meantime does not cost you that. With
.B \-\-show
the password is printed instead.
.TP
.RB [ ls ]
.I PATTERN
List entries. With a pattern, only those whose name, username or url contains
it, case insensitively. Notes are not searched.
.TP
.BI mv " OLD NEW"
Rename an entry, carrying every field across.
.TP
.BI rm " NAME"
Remove an entry.
.TP
.B gen
Generate a password and print it, without touching the vault.
.TP
.B passwd
Change the master password and re\-encrypt the vault under it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-vault " PATH"
Use this vault instead of the default.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
On
.BR ls ,
emit machine readable output. Passwords are never included, because this is the
output people redirect into files and pipe into other programs.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.hoard/vault
The vault. Created mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.hoard/vault.bak
The previous contents, kept on each write. Note that after
.B passwd
this backup is still readable with the old master password.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOARD_VAULT
Path to the vault, overriding the default.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error the user should see, such as a wrong password or a missing entry.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH VAULT FORMAT
.nf
b"HOARD1\\n" + header_json + b"\\n" + ciphertext
.fi
.PP
The header is JSON holding
.IR kdf ", " m ", " t ", " p ", " salt " and " nonce .
The ciphertext is the vault JSON sealed with ChaCha20\-Poly1305 under a key
derived by Argon2id, with the header as associated data.
.PP
Argon2id is taken from python3\-cryptography where that version provides it and
from python3\-argon2 otherwise. Both wrap the same reference implementation and
produce identical keys, so a vault opens on either.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hoard-gui (1)
.SH AUTHOR
konnen916
